\use{document}
\use{chem}

\title{Reactions with \texttt{chem}}
\author{The texish project}
\maketitle

\section{Reaction equations}

A reaction is a row of species joined by \texttt{\\plus} and the reaction arrow
\texttt{\\rxnarrow}, with atom counts written as subscripts (\texttt{\\dn}) and
ionic charges as superscripts (\texttt{\\up}). The leading number of a species is
its stoichiometric coefficient, set at full size. The combustion of methane:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular CH\dn{4} \plus 2O\dn{2} \rxnarrow CO\dn{2} \plus 2H\dn{2}O}
\bigskip

\noindent A dissociation, showing ionic charges with \texttt{\\up}:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular NaCl \rxnarrow Na\up{+} \plus Cl\up{-}}
\bigskip

\noindent Because each species is just an inline box, a drawn \texttt{\\picture}
structure can stand in place of a formula and the same \texttt{\\plus} and
\texttt{\\rxnarrow} lay out the equation.

\vfill\eject
